Subject: [PATCH 08106/11362] Correction: 102d4cb33c shipped ~300 lines of
 instrumentation its message did not mention

That commit describes a scoped win-transition reachability fix. It added 343 lines to
arc_competition_agent.py, of which roughly 30 are that fix -- the other ~300 are the
action-provenance instrumentation authored by a concurrent workflow, which had deliberately NOT
committed them because it judged the artifact-freshness cost too broad to take on unasked.

Nothing is broken and nothing was lost: the instrumentation is default-OFF with three independent
inertness proofs (AST diff, a three-arm off/ON/off runtime identity check whose 240-action traces
all hash to 9ccc8c2e6bc00b3e, and a mutation-proven unit test), and its module and tests landed
intentionally in 721bb8f772. The defect is in the RECORD: a reader of 102d4cb33c would not learn
that the scored agent gained an instrumentation layer.

THE MITIGATION THAT FAILED IS A COMMON ONE. The commit was staged with explicit per-file paths
precisely to avoid sweeping in another agent's work. That gives FILE-level isolation and NO
LINE-level isolation -- `git add <path>` stages the file's whole working-tree content, and this
file already carried the other workflow's restored changes. `git diff --cached --numstat` was run
first, but inspected only for deletions, so +343 against a thirty-line change went unquestioned.

Working rule added to ops/changelog.md: on a shared tree, read `git diff --cached --stat` for
VOLUME as well as deletions. A line count that does not match the change you believe you are
making is the signal that another writer's work is inside your staged file.

Recorded rather than amended -- the subject line is immutable, the same reasoning applied on
2026-07-31 to a "triples the rate" subject that could not be fixed in place.

